# I considered whether "will" shifts between S3 ("the will has an influence only on present actions") and C/S4 ("the willing of that obligation"). Both take willing as an act of volition; they differ in its object (an action versus an obligation), not in what "will" means.
# Inside each claim, "will" keeps one sense across its statements. A3 joins willing_action and will_present_only with volition in the same sense, so A3 is not a bridge.
# A1 joins "the act of the mind exprest by a promise" with "the act of the mind which enters into a promise". The text treats these as the same act, in one sense.
